Comparing the two docs, the CSV spec only seeds one deliberate edge case (a blank Actual_Value for missing-value testing) — but the formula doc requires the assistant to correctly handle four other edge cases that currently have no planned test data:

Main gap — edge-case coverage in the synthetic data itself. The formula doc (Division by Zero, Non-Numeric Values, Negative Results sections) needs seeded rows for:

Zero values — e.g. a Budget_Value = 0 row (for Percentage Variance ÷0), a Revenue = 0 row (Gross Margin ÷0), a category set summing to Total Dollar Variance = 0 (Variance Contribution), and a prior-period value = 0 (PoP/YoY).
Negative values — a negative Revenue row (Gross Margin's "invalid input" case), a negative Budget_Value row (Percentage Variance's "explanatory note" case), and a negative prior-period value (PoP/YoY explanatory note case).
Non-numeric values — the CSV spec says invalid text/symbols get "rejected... on ingestion," but the formula doc requires the assistant to detect and cite a non-numeric value during a calculation. If bad values are stripped at ingestion, they never reach the queryable table for the assistant to report on. This needs a decision: either (a) malformed rows are quarantined but still retained/flagged for the assistant to cite, or (b) at least one row is intentionally left with a non-numeric string (like the missing-value blank) so the pipeline can be tested end-to-end.
Secondary gap — where gaps are placed. The formula doc requires quarterly/yearly aggregations to surface which specific month is missing when a quarter or year total can't be computed. Right now only one month (Rent Expense, Jan 2025) is blank — worth deciding whether that gap should deliberately fall inside a quarter/year that gets tested, and whether additional gaps are needed to exercise the "missing month inside an aggregate" path specifically (as opposed to just the single-month lookup path).

One clarification, not a gap: the formula doc's own citation examples ("Column C", "Column E", etc.) don't line up with the CSV spec's actual column order (Actual_Value=E, Budget_Value=F) — but the formula doc explicitly says column layout is "out of scope" for it, so those are just illustrative placeholders, not a real conflict. Worth a quick confirmation with the BA so nobody mistakes them as binding.

